  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A warming trend begins tomorrow and peaks Friday, \nwith a cold air wedge building in for the weekend. \n \nA broad sub-tropical ridge now over the Gulf coast states will\nmove east and build north into the interior Mid-Atlantic the \nnext couple of days. Our recent period of cooler and dry \nnortherly flow will be replaced by southwest winds/WAA tomorrow \nthrough Friday. This will mean an increase in temperatures, with\nsome locations reaching the mid 80s to lower 90s in the \nPiedmont and around larger urban areas like Roanoke tomorrow \nthrough Friday. \n\nIt looks like a brief CAD event develops for the weekend. Expect \nIncreasing clouds and rain chances to suppress temperatures on \nSaturday, with frontal passages ushering in even cooler weather \nfor Sunday into Monday, with highs in the 60s and 70s. The wedge\nlooks to break down on Tuesday with sunshine \nreturning...however cooler temperatures remain. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The current period of dry and clear weather ends \nFriday into the weekend with increasing chances for rain. \n\nCooler and dry northerly flow will be replaced by southwest \nwinds and slowly increasing moisture through Friday. By then, \nthe sub-tropical ridge will be suppressed as a mean trough axis\napproaches from the west. Most of the cyclonic energy will stay\nto our north, but it will help drive a surface low and frontal \nsystem into the area for the weekend. Several ensemble members \nhold precip off until Friday evening, as it will take some time \nfor the atmosphere to regain moisture. Once it does, it looks \nlike some pretty significant rainfall totals could be realized \nover the weekend. A lot of this rain will fall in showers and \nstorms associated with a couple cold frontal passages, with the \nlast one exiting to our south sometime Monday.",
